WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:20.040
Let's begin.

00:20.040 --> 00:22.360
Threats in the wake of bin Laden's death have spiked.

00:22.360 --> 00:23.680
Baman in Somalia.

00:23.680 --> 00:24.680
Police pepper spray.

00:24.680 --> 00:25.680
Vicious cartels.

00:25.680 --> 00:26.680
Caustic cruise lines.

00:26.680 --> 00:28.240
The sun will decay.

00:28.240 --> 00:29.240
65 dead.

00:29.760 --> 00:30.760
Cyber attacks.

00:30.760 --> 00:31.760
Drug war.

00:31.760 --> 00:32.760
Mass destruction.

00:32.760 --> 00:33.760
Tornado.

00:33.760 --> 00:34.760
Recession.

00:34.760 --> 00:35.760
Default.

00:35.760 --> 00:36.760
Doomsday.

00:36.760 --> 00:37.760
Egypt.

00:37.760 --> 00:38.760
Syria.

00:38.760 --> 00:39.760
Crisis.

00:39.760 --> 00:40.760
Death.

00:40.760 --> 00:41.760
Disaster.

00:41.760 --> 00:42.760
Oh my god.

00:42.760 --> 00:43.760
So those are just a few of the clips I collected over the last six months.

00:43.760 --> 00:48.000
Could have easily been the last six days or the last six years.

00:48.000 --> 00:53.660
The point is that the news media preferentially feeds us negative stories because that's what

00:53.660 --> 00:55.640
our minds pay attention to.

00:55.640 --> 00:58.560
And there's a very good reason for that.

00:58.560 --> 01:03.200
Every second of every day, our senses bring in way too much data than we can possibly

01:03.200 --> 01:05.240
process in our brains.

01:05.240 --> 01:10.200
And because nothing is more important to us than survival, the first stop of all of that

01:10.200 --> 01:14.640
data is an ancient sliver of the temporal lobe called the amygdala.

01:14.640 --> 01:20.360
Now the amygdala is our early warning detector, our danger detector.

01:20.360 --> 01:24.440
It sorts and scours through all of the information, looking for anything in the environment that

01:24.440 --> 01:26.240
might harm us.

01:26.240 --> 01:32.420
So given a dozen news stories, we will preferentially look at the negative news.

01:32.420 --> 01:38.680
And that old newspaper saying, if it bleeds, it leads, is very true.

01:38.680 --> 01:43.320
So given all of our digital devices that are bringing all the negative news to us seven

01:43.320 --> 01:48.400
days a week, 24 hours a day, it's no wonder that we're pessimistic.

01:48.400 --> 01:53.880
It's no wonder that people think that the world is getting worse.

01:53.880 --> 01:57.240
But perhaps that's not the case.

01:57.240 --> 02:04.360
Perhaps instead it's the distortions brought to us of what's really going on.

02:04.360 --> 02:09.440
Perhaps the tremendous progress we've made over the last century by a series of forces

02:09.440 --> 02:15.960
are in fact accelerating to a point that we have the potential in the next three decades

02:15.960 --> 02:18.980
to create a world of abundance.

02:18.980 --> 02:23.020
Now I'm not saying we don't have our set of problems.

02:23.020 --> 02:28.140
Climate crisis, species extinction, water and energy shortage, we surely do.

02:28.140 --> 02:33.820
And as humans, we are far better at seeing the problems way in advance, but ultimately

02:33.820 --> 02:37.220
we knock them down.

02:37.220 --> 02:43.660
So let's look at what this last century has been to see where we're going.

02:43.660 --> 02:48.480
Over the last 100 years, the average human lifespan has more than doubled.

02:48.480 --> 02:53.400
Average per capita income adjusted for inflation around the world has tripled.

02:53.400 --> 02:57.880
Childhood mortality has come down a factor of 10.

02:57.880 --> 03:01.680
Add to that the cost of food, electricity, transportation, communications have dropped

03:01.680 --> 03:05.520
10 to 1,000 fold.

03:05.520 --> 03:09.840
Steve Pinker has showed us that in fact we're living during the most peaceful time ever

03:09.840 --> 03:12.140
in human history.

03:12.140 --> 03:17.360
And Charles Kenney, that global literacy has gone from 25 percent to over 80 percent in

03:17.360 --> 03:20.020
the last 130 years.

03:20.020 --> 03:24.060
We truly are living in an extraordinary time.

03:24.060 --> 03:29.040
And many people forget this, and we keep setting our expectations higher and higher.

03:29.040 --> 03:32.200
In fact, we redefine what poverty means.

03:32.200 --> 03:37.440
Think of this, in America today, the majority of people under the poverty line still have

03:37.440 --> 03:42.360
electricity, water, toilets, refrigerators, television, mobile phones, air conditioning

03:42.360 --> 03:44.740
and cars.

03:44.740 --> 03:49.060
The wealthiest robber barons of the last century, the emperors on this planet, could have never

03:49.060 --> 03:54.860
dreamed of such luxuries.

03:54.860 --> 04:02.940
Underpinning much of this is technology, and of late, exponentially growing technologies.

04:02.940 --> 04:07.460
My good friend Ray Kurzweil showed that any tool that becomes an information technology

04:07.460 --> 04:14.700
jumps on this curve, on Moore's Law, and experiences price performance doubling every 12 to 24

04:14.700 --> 04:16.180
months.

04:16.180 --> 04:19.600
That's why the cell phone in your pocket is literally a million times cheaper and a thousand

04:19.600 --> 04:21.860
times faster than a supercomputer of the 70s.

04:21.860 --> 04:23.820
Now look at this curve.

04:23.820 --> 04:26.140
This is Moore's Law over the last hundred years.

04:26.140 --> 04:28.520
I want you to notice two things from this curve.

04:28.520 --> 04:34.940
Number one, how smooth it is through good time and bad time, war time and peace time,

04:34.940 --> 04:38.700
recession, depression, and boom time.

04:38.700 --> 04:43.980
This is the result of faster computers being used to build faster computers.

04:43.980 --> 04:47.640
It doesn't slow for any of our grand challenges.

04:47.640 --> 04:52.680
And also, even though it's plotted on a log curve on the left, it's curving upwards.

04:52.680 --> 04:58.180
The rate at which the technology is getting faster is itself getting faster.

04:58.180 --> 05:04.740
And on this curve, riding on Moore's Law, are a set of extraordinarily powerful technologies

05:04.740 --> 05:06.900
available to all of us.

05:06.900 --> 05:11.900
Cloud computing, what my friends at Autodesk call infinite computing, sensors and networks,

05:11.900 --> 05:16.500
robotics, 3D printing, which is the ability to democratize and distribute personalized

05:16.500 --> 05:23.980
production around the planet, synthetic biology, fuels, vaccines and foods, digital medicine,

05:23.980 --> 05:26.420
nanomaterials and AI.

05:26.420 --> 05:31.740
How many of you saw the winning of Jeopardy by IBM's Watson?

05:31.740 --> 05:32.740
That was epic.

05:32.740 --> 05:38.220
In fact, I scoured the headlines looking for the best headline in the newspaper I could

05:38.220 --> 05:42.820
and I love this, Watson vanquishes human opponents.

05:42.820 --> 05:44.980
Jeopardy is not an easy game.

05:44.980 --> 05:47.620
It's about the nuance of human language.

05:47.620 --> 05:52.340
And imagine if you would, AIs like this on the cloud available to every person with a

05:52.340 --> 05:55.680
cell phone.

05:55.680 --> 06:00.220
Four years ago here at TED, Ray Kurzweil and I started a new university called Singularity

06:00.220 --> 06:01.540
University.

06:01.540 --> 06:06.660
And we teach our students all of these technologies, in particular, how they can be used to solve

06:06.660 --> 06:09.700
humanity's grand challenges.

06:09.700 --> 06:15.220
And every year we ask them to start a company or product or service that can affect positively

06:15.220 --> 06:19.460
the lives of a billion people within a decade.

06:19.460 --> 06:20.460
Think about that.

06:20.460 --> 06:25.180
The fact that literally a group of students can touch the lives of a billion people today.

06:25.180 --> 06:27.660
30 years ago that would have sounded ludicrous.

06:27.660 --> 06:32.380
Today we can point at dozens of companies that have done just that.

06:33.380 --> 06:39.980
So when I think about creating abundance, it's not about creating a life of luxury for

06:39.980 --> 06:41.220
a planet.

06:41.220 --> 06:44.540
It's about creating a life of possibility.

06:44.540 --> 06:49.340
It is about taking that which was scarce and making it abundant.

06:49.340 --> 06:57.840
You see, scarcity is contextual and technology is a resource liberating force.

06:57.840 --> 07:00.220
Let me give you an example.

07:00.220 --> 07:04.500
So this is a story of Napoleon III in the mid-1800s.

07:04.500 --> 07:07.220
He's the dude on the left.

07:07.220 --> 07:12.180
He invited over to dinner the King of Siam.

07:12.180 --> 07:16.700
All of Napoleon's troops were fed with silver utensils.

07:16.700 --> 07:18.260
Napoleon himself with gold utensils.

07:18.260 --> 07:22.580
But the King of Siam, he was fed with aluminum utensils.

07:22.580 --> 07:30.200
You see, aluminum was the most valuable metal on the planet, worth more than gold and platinum.

07:30.200 --> 07:35.000
It's the reason that the tip of the Washington Monument is made of aluminum.

07:35.000 --> 07:41.920
You see, even though aluminum is 8.3% of the earth by mass, it doesn't come as a pure metal.

07:41.920 --> 07:45.380
It's all bound by oxygen and silicates.

07:45.380 --> 07:51.280
But then a technology of electrolysis came along and literally made aluminum so cheap

07:51.280 --> 07:54.200
that we use it with throwaway mentality.

07:54.200 --> 07:57.940
So let's project this analogy going forward.

07:57.940 --> 08:00.900
We think about energy scarcity.

08:00.900 --> 08:06.880
Ladies and gentlemen, we are on a planet that is bathed with 5,000 times more energy than

08:06.880 --> 08:10.100
we use in a year.

08:10.100 --> 08:16.380
Sixteen terawatts of energy hit the earth's surface every 88 minutes.

08:16.380 --> 08:19.940
It's not about being scarce, it's about accessibility.

08:19.940 --> 08:22.100
And there's good news here.

08:22.100 --> 08:28.420
For the first time this year, the cost of solar-generated electricity is 50% that of

08:28.420 --> 08:33.700
diesel-generated electricity in India, 8.8 rupees versus 17 rupees.

08:33.700 --> 08:36.580
The cost of solar dropped 50% last year.

08:36.580 --> 08:40.980
Last month, MIT put out a study showing that by the end of this decade, in the sunny parts

08:40.980 --> 08:45.700
of the United States, solar electricity would be 6 cents a kilowatt hour compared to 15

08:45.700 --> 08:48.700
cents as a national average.

08:48.700 --> 08:53.980
And if we have abundant energy, we also have abundant water.

08:53.980 --> 08:59.260
Now, we talk about water wars.

08:59.260 --> 09:04.820
Do you remember when Carl Sagan turned the Voyager spacecraft back towards the earth

09:04.820 --> 09:07.140
in 1990 after it just passed Saturn?

09:07.140 --> 09:08.140
He took a famous photo.

09:08.140 --> 09:10.380
What was it called?

09:10.380 --> 09:12.740
A pale blue dot.

09:12.740 --> 09:15.380
Because we live on a water planet.

09:15.380 --> 09:18.180
We live on a planet 70% covered by water.

09:18.180 --> 09:24.060
Yes, 97.5% is salt water, 2% is ice, and we fight over a half a percent of the water

09:24.060 --> 09:25.460
on this planet.

09:25.460 --> 09:27.860
But here, too, there is hope.

09:27.860 --> 09:34.460
And there is technology coming online, not 10, 20 years from now, right now.

09:34.460 --> 09:37.300
There's nanotechnology coming on, nanomaterials.

09:37.300 --> 09:41.420
And the conversation I had with Dean Kamen this morning, one of the great DIY innovators

09:41.420 --> 09:45.520
I'd like to share with you, he gave me permission to do so.

09:45.520 --> 09:49.440
This technology, called Slingshot, that many of you may have heard of, it's the size of

09:49.440 --> 09:51.500
a small dorm room refrigerator.

09:51.500 --> 09:56.560
It's able to generate 1,000 liters of clean drinking water a day out of any source, salt

09:56.560 --> 10:03.120
water, polluted water, latrine, at less than 2 cents a liter.

10:03.120 --> 10:08.580
The chairman of Coca-Cola has just agreed to do a major test of hundreds of units of

10:08.580 --> 10:09.920
this in the developing world.

10:09.920 --> 10:15.960
And if that pans out, which of every confidence it will, Coca-Cola will deploy this globally

10:15.960 --> 10:20.280
to 206 countries around the planet.

10:20.280 --> 10:26.860
This is the kind of innovation, empowered by this technology, that exists today.

10:26.860 --> 10:28.560
And we've seen this in cell phones.

10:28.560 --> 10:33.600
My goodness, we're going to hit 70% penetration of cell phones in the developing world by

10:33.600 --> 10:35.840
the end of 2013.

10:35.840 --> 10:40.520
Think about it, that a Maasai warrior on a cell phone in the middle of Kenya has better

10:40.520 --> 10:45.880
mobile com than President Reagan did 25 years ago.

10:45.880 --> 10:49.320
And if they're on a smartphone on Google, they've got access to more knowledge and information

10:49.320 --> 10:51.480
than President Clinton did 15 years ago.

10:51.480 --> 10:54.720
They're living in a world of information and communication abundance that no one could

10:54.720 --> 10:58.160
have ever predicted.

10:58.160 --> 11:02.440
Better than that, the things that you and I spent tens and hundreds of thousands of

11:02.440 --> 11:10.760
dollars for, GPS, HD video and still images, libraries of books and music, medical diagnostic

11:10.760 --> 11:20.600
technology are now literally dematerializing and demonetizing into your cell phone.

11:20.600 --> 11:25.240
Probably the best part of it is what's coming down the pike in health.

11:25.240 --> 11:29.800
Last month, I had the pleasure of announcing with Qualcomm Foundation something called

11:29.800 --> 11:33.200
the $10 million Qualcomm TriQuarter XPRIZE.

11:33.200 --> 11:37.220
We're challenging teams around the world to basically combine these technologies into

11:37.220 --> 11:42.160
a mobile device that you can speak to because it's got AI, you can cough on it, you can

11:42.160 --> 11:46.440
do a finger blood trick, and to win, it needs to be able to diagnose you better than a team

11:46.440 --> 11:50.120
of board certified doctors.

11:50.120 --> 11:55.300
So literally, imagine this device in the middle of the developing world where there are no

11:55.300 --> 11:56.300
doctors.

11:56.300 --> 12:01.340
1.5% of the disease burden and 1.3% of the healthcare workers.

12:01.340 --> 12:06.420
When this device sequences an RNA or DNA virus that it doesn't recognize, it calls the CDC

12:06.420 --> 12:12.180
and prevents the pandemic from happening in the first place.

12:12.180 --> 12:16.940
But here, here is the biggest force for bringing about a world of abundance.

12:16.940 --> 12:20.200
I call it the rising billion.

12:20.200 --> 12:23.040
So the white lines here are population.

12:23.040 --> 12:26.040
We just passed the 7 billion mark on earth.

12:26.040 --> 12:31.280
And by the way, the biggest protection against a population explosion is making the world

12:31.280 --> 12:33.840
educated and healthy.

12:33.840 --> 12:41.440
In 2010, we had just short of 2 billion people online connected.

12:41.440 --> 12:47.080
By 2020, that's going from 2 billion to 5 billion internet users.

12:47.080 --> 12:52.640
Three billion new minds who have never been heard from before are connecting to the global

12:53.240 --> 12:55.240
conversation.

12:55.240 --> 12:56.240
What will these people want?

12:56.240 --> 12:57.240
What will they consume?

12:57.240 --> 12:58.760
What will they desire?

12:58.760 --> 13:03.320
And rather than having economic shutdown, we're about to have the biggest economic injection

13:03.320 --> 13:04.320
ever.

13:04.320 --> 13:11.960
These people represent tens of trillions of dollars injected into the global economy.

13:11.960 --> 13:16.360
And they will get healthier by using a tricorder, and they'll become better educated by using

13:16.360 --> 13:22.860
the Khan Academy, and by literally being able to use 3D printing and infinite computing

13:22.860 --> 13:26.200
more productive than ever before.

13:26.200 --> 13:34.440
So what could 3 billion rising, healthy, educated, productive members of humanity bring to us?

13:34.440 --> 13:37.780
How about a set of voices that have never been heard from before?

13:37.780 --> 13:42.480
What about giving the oppressed, wherever they might be, the voice to be heard and the

13:42.480 --> 13:45.920
voice to act for the first time ever?

13:45.920 --> 13:48.960
What will these 3 billion people bring?

13:48.960 --> 13:52.520
What about contributions we can't even predict?

13:52.520 --> 13:58.160
The one thing I've learned at the XPRIZE is that small teams driven by their passion with

13:58.160 --> 14:03.480
a clearer focus can do extraordinary things, things that large corporations and governments

14:03.480 --> 14:05.760
could only do in the past.

14:05.760 --> 14:11.360
Let me share and close with a story that really got me excited.

14:11.360 --> 14:13.320
There is a program that some of you might have heard of.

14:13.320 --> 14:15.460
It's a game called Foldit.

14:15.500 --> 14:19.260
It came out of the University of Washington in Seattle.

14:19.260 --> 14:26.000
And this is a game where individuals can actually take a sequence of amino acids and figure

14:26.000 --> 14:29.020
out how the protein is going to fold.

14:29.020 --> 14:31.680
And how it folds dictates its structure and its functionality.

14:31.680 --> 14:35.380
It's very important for research and medicine, and up until now, it's been a supercomputer

14:35.380 --> 14:37.680
problem.

14:37.680 --> 14:42.100
And this game has been played by university professors and so forth, and it's literally

14:42.100 --> 14:46.500
hundreds of thousands of people came online and started playing it, and it showed that

14:46.500 --> 14:51.220
in fact today, the human pattern recognition machinery is better at folding proteins than

14:51.220 --> 14:53.260
the best computers.

14:53.260 --> 14:58.740
And when these individuals went and looked at who is the best protein folder in the world,

14:58.740 --> 15:04.220
it wasn't an MIT professor, it wasn't a Caltech student, it was a person from England, from

15:04.220 --> 15:12.620
Manchester, a woman, who during the day was an executive assistant at a rehab clinic,

15:12.620 --> 15:18.100
and at night was the world's best protein folder.

15:18.100 --> 15:25.820
Ladies and gentlemen, what gives me tremendous confidence in the future is the fact that

15:25.820 --> 15:32.020
we are now more empowered as individuals to take on the grand challenges of this planet.

15:32.020 --> 15:37.540
We have the tools with this exponential technology, we have the passion of the DIY innovator,

15:37.540 --> 15:43.600
we have the capital of the techno-philanthropist, and we have three billion new minds coming

15:43.600 --> 15:50.680
online to work with us to solve the grand challenges, to do that which we must do.

15:50.680 --> 15:53.620
We are living into an extraordinary decades ahead.

15:53.620 --> 15:54.620
Thank you.

15:54.620 --> 15:55.620
Thank you.

15:55.620 --> 15:56.120
Thank you.

